  <!-- ========== DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The United States issued a Saturday deadline (July 12 EOD, confirmed by three senior US officials per Axios July 10) demanding Iran renounce the July 6–7 Hormuz commercial-shipping attacks and declare the strait unconditionally open to all vessels. Iranian FM Abbas Araghchi landed in Muscat on July 11 for scheduled talks with Omani FM Sayyid Badr al-Busaidi — IRNA and Xinhua both confirmed the trip active as of July 11 morning, with the agenda described as "shipping safety and regional security." → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>The Islamabad Memorandum — initialled June 14 with Witkoff (US) and Araghchi (Iran), confirmed by VP Vance June 15, and elaborated at the Lucerne Summit June 21–22 — was declared void by Trump on July 8 following Iranian naval and drone attacks on three commercial vessels in the strait July 6–7, per Reuters and AFP. Iranian FM Spokesperson Baghaei stated July 10 that Tehran "has not requested negotiations" and characterized the attacks as a matter of "sovereign navigation rights." Pakistani and Qatari intermediaries remained in contact after the collapse; Axios July 10 reported Iranian diplomatic sources communicated to US counterparts that Tehran "wants to keep talking." → moved <span class="cite">F11</span></p>

      <p>Knesset legal adviser Sagit Afik formally recommended July 17 as the Knesset's dissolution date; Speaker Ohana and Coalition Whip Katz both confirmed the July 17 timetable in separate public statements to Israeli media by July 11. The coalition completed a legislative sprint through July 10 — haredi-linked exemption bills and judicial appointments — racing to close the pre-dissolution agenda (Haaretz, July 5). All coalition parties and the legal adviser publicly confirmed the expected election date as October 27, the latest permissible date under Israeli electoral law. → moved <span class="cite">F7</span>, <span class="cite">F10</span></p>

      <p>Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei — elevated March 9 after his father's assassination February 28 — was absent from Ali Khamenei's state funeral July 3–10, the highest-obligation public event for a sitting Supreme Leader by political convention. Time (July 5) reported "Iran's new leader has yet to appear publicly"; CNN July 4 live funeral coverage cited "unknown whereabouts." Iranian governance since February has been conducted via written directives circulated through IRNA, with no visual or in-person confirmation of the new Supreme Leader at any public event during 134 days of the crisis. → moved <span class="cite">F12</span></p>

      <p>CENTCOM MCM (mine-countermeasure) vessels were reported by Bloomberg (July 7) as making measurable progress in strait clearance before the July 6–7 attacks disrupted the clearance corridor. IMF PortWatch data confirms a single-day transit peak of 54 on June 24 — the only day approaching the threshold that would have resolved F4 YES — but no 7-day average reached 50 within any consecutive window before July 2; post-July 6 transit counts retreated to approximately 22/day per July 9 tracking, with the clearance corridor disrupted by renewed Iranian interdiction. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:33%;">Escalation 33%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:43%;">Protracted 43%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:24%;">De-esc 24%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (33%):</strong> Iranian non-compliance with Saturday deadline triggers US military response, Hormuz becomes a contested warzone, oil shock deepens, and Iran strikes regional US bases — driven by F9 failing (p=78%) combined with the US maintaining its ultimatum posture.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (43%):</strong> Araghchi Muscat mediation produces an ambiguous de-escalation that falls short of explicit renunciation — buying days but not resolution — leaving Hormuz transits suppressed below 30/day for months while F11 remains unresolved through September.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (24%):</strong> Iran issues compliant state-media statement before July 17, Muscat talks produce an interim framework (F8 YES + F9 YES), Pakistan/Qatar convene a replacement Lucerne-type round (F11 building), and transit recovery begins within two weeks.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Tasnim July 10–11 coverage of the Araghchi Oman visit frames the trip as a "good-will initiative on shipping safety" — the standard Iranian state-media vocabulary for bilateral talks conducted under diplomatic pressure. No IRNA bulletin in the last 48 hours references the US Saturday deadline or the July 6–7 attacks by name; the closest public acknowledgment was FM Spokesperson Baghaei's July 10 presser characterizing Iran's Hormuz posture as "one of sovereign navigation rights, consistent with international law." → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>IRIB (state television) and Press TV coverage of Ali Khamenei's state funeral July 3–10 ran 96-hour ceremonial programming: narratives of "steadfastness" (استواری) and "continuity of the revolutionary path" dominated editorial framing across all state channels. No IRIB broadcast or state-media outlet mentioned Mojtaba Khamenei's location or absence from the proceedings; Kayhan (July 8) characterized the funeral as "a testament to the Islamic Republic's institutional resilience," deploying the event as a frame for the new Supreme Leader's legitimacy without any physical appearance by him. → supports <span class="cite">F12</span> without move</p>

      <p>Fars News July 9 published a feature on "Strait sovereignty" citing an unnamed IRGC source claiming "the defensive Hormuz operations were fully in accordance with international law and were provoked by unlawful US naval presence." No Iranian state outlet reported the July 6–7 attacks as a violation of the Islamabad Memorandum; IRNA last characterized that agreement on June 16 as "still the framework for negotiations." The 25-day gap between IRNA's June 16 framing and Baghaei's July 10 statement that Tehran "has not requested negotiations" marks a structural break in the official Iranian diplomatic communication track, without an intermediary public explanation offered by any state outlet. → supports <span class="cite">F11</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

